# SYNOPSIS

```text
size[4] Tauth tag[2] afid[4] uname[s] aname[s]
size[4] Rauth tag[2] aqid[13]

size[4] Tattach tag[2] fid[4] afid[4] uname[s] aname[s]
size[4] Rattach tag[2] qid[13]
```

# DESCRIPTION

The `attach` request introduces a user to the server and gives the client a fid for the root of a file tree.
`Tauth` is type 102, `Rauth` 103, `Tattach` 104 and `Rattach` 105.
<uname> names the user, <aname> chooses the tree, and <afid> is a fid made by `Tauth`, or `NOFID`, `0xffffffff`, for none.
If the attach succeeds, <fid> refers to the tree's root, and <qid> is the root's.
9Px's `Tattach` is 9P2000's: 9P2000.L's adds a numeric user, `n_uname[4]`, which 9Px does not have, and a 9P2000.L client is answered with plain 9P2000 (see version(5)), so it sends 9P2000's.

A VectraOS server refuses a `Tattach` before `Tversion` (see version(5)), and one whose <afid> is not `NOFID` with `operation not supported`.
<fid> must not be `NOFID` or in use on the connection, and the connection must have room for it, 256 fids at once; otherwise the answer is `fid already in use`.
The file server then attaches <aname>, and its error, if any, is the answer, in which case <fid> stays unused.

What <aname> means is each server's.
fsd(4) serves a branch, a snapshot or its dump view by the name, and bootfs(4) the directory the name is a path to; every other server has one tree and takes only the empty name, answering any other `file does not exist`.
The fid remembers the root it was attached at: `..` there is the root itself, and no walk from it leaves the tree (see walk(5)).
A connection may attach as often as it likes, to different trees and as different users.

<uname> is advisory.
A server that has users, such as fsd(4), checks every later request on the fid and on fids walked from it as that user; the others ignore it.
Nothing stops a client from naming anyone until the system has a key server.

The `auth` request makes <afid> a fid for an authentication protocol, with the <uname> and <aname> of the attach to follow, and in Plan 9 the reply's <aqid> is a file of type `QTAUTH` that the client reads and writes to run the protocol.
No VectraOS server requires authentication, and every server answers `Tauth` with `operation not supported`, as a Plan 9 server that needs none answers it with an `Rerror`.
Tokens presented in `Tauth` or `Tattach` are planned, with the key server (`docs/02` §3.4).

VectraOS's client sends <afid> `NOFID`, and as <fid> the next number of a count that starts at 1 after `Tversion` and is never wound back.
It attaches as the program's user, from its spawn message's `user=`, or as `none` when the program has none; over TCP it attaches as `vectra`, since a Plan 9 server refuses `none` before a connection has authenticated.
It never sends `Tauth`.

# EXAMPLES

The exchanges `tests/host/p9_server_test.c` makes and checks, written in Plan 9's trace form, with a server whose file server knows `docs` but has no authentication:

```text
Tattach tag 1 fid 1 afid -1 uname '' aname 'docs'
Rattach tag 1 qid (0000000000000002 0 d)
Tattach tag 1 fid 1 afid -1 uname '' aname ''
Rerror tag 1 ename fid already in use
Tattach tag 1 fid 9 afid 3 uname '' aname ''
Rerror tag 1 ename operation not supported
```

